1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,330
There are a few hundred
billion stars in our galaxy.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:05,690
And one of them is our nearest neighbour.

3
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:08,309
Proxima Centauri.

4
00:00:08,619 --> 00:00:11,509
It's about four and a
quarter light-years away.

5
00:00:12,415 --> 00:00:13,645
Which sounds close.

6
00:00:14,601 --> 00:00:18,091
Until you realise that, with
the spacecraft we have today,

7
00:00:18,181 --> 00:00:20,931
getting there would take
tens of thousands of years.

8
00:00:21,737 --> 00:00:23,627
But here's the interesting part.

9
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:27,081
We actually know how to
send a spacecraft out of the

10
00:00:27,161 --> 00:00:30,811
Solar System and put it on a
course for Proxima Centauri.

11
00:00:31,593 --> 00:00:33,903
We have the rockets.
We have the computers.

12
00:00:34,813 --> 00:00:36,543
We have the navigation technology.

13
00:00:37,493 --> 00:00:41,123
We even have spacecraft already
flying through interstellar space.

14
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:46,143
So... could we actually send a spacecraft
to another star with today's technology?

15
00:00:47,085 --> 00:00:48,575
Well... sort of.

16
00:00:49,314 --> 00:00:51,544
Because once you look at
what it would have to do...

17
00:00:51,994 --> 00:00:54,164
things get ridiculous very quickly.

18
00:00:56,424 --> 00:00:59,614
Let's start with the
obvious problem. Distance.

19
00:01:00,703 --> 00:01:03,265
Four point two four light-years.

20
00:01:03,403 --> 00:01:05,013
That's roughly forty trillion kilometres.

21
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:10,509
Our fastest spacecraft aren't even
remotely close to the speed of light.

22
00:01:11,308 --> 00:01:14,608
Voyager one, the fastest thing we
have ever sent out of the Solar

23
00:01:14,688 --> 00:01:19,018
System, is travelling at roughly
seventeen kilometres per second.

24
00:01:19,831 --> 00:01:21,741
That sounds fast. It's not.

25
00:01:22,636 --> 00:01:25,176
At that speed, reaching
Proxima Centauri would

26
00:01:25,256 --> 00:01:27,866
take roughly seventy-five thousand years.

27
00:01:29,881 --> 00:01:30,881
Seventy-five thousand years.

28
00:01:30,961 --> 00:01:34,971
The first pyramids were built only
about five thousand years ago.

29
00:01:35,758 --> 00:01:37,448
So this isn't a long mission.

30
00:01:38,278 --> 00:01:40,298
It's sending a machine
into space and hoping

31
00:01:40,378 --> 00:01:42,828
it still works when it finally gets there.

32
00:01:43,493 --> 00:01:45,683
And that is a completely
different problem.

33
00:01:48,203 --> 00:01:52,033
Think about the machines around
you. Your phone. Your car.

34
00:01:52,739 --> 00:01:56,389
How many of them do you expect to work
perfectly in the year ten thousand?

35
00:01:57,635 --> 00:01:58,835
Probably none.

36
00:01:59,562 --> 00:02:03,032
And we're asking a spacecraft to
survive not ten thousand years...

37
00:02:03,182 --> 00:02:05,809
but seventy-five thousand.

38
00:02:06,259 --> 00:02:10,309
The Voyager probes are among the
longest-lived machines we have ever built.

39
00:02:11,019 --> 00:02:12,649
They have been running for decades.

40
00:02:13,631 --> 00:02:15,271
And they're already
having systems switched

41
00:02:15,351 --> 00:02:17,621
off to conserve their remaining power.

42
00:02:18,421 --> 00:02:22,021
An interstellar probe would have
to survive radiation, extreme

43
00:02:22,101 --> 00:02:26,171
temperatures, and thousands of other
little things that can go wrong.

44
00:02:26,901 --> 00:02:31,311
And there is one particularly nasty
problem... you can't repair it.

45
00:02:32,019 --> 00:02:35,589
There is no engineer who can travel
there and reboot the computer.

46
00:02:36,319 --> 00:02:38,639
The spacecraft has to be
designed to repair itself...

47
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:41,429
or survive without repairs.

48
00:02:43,859 --> 00:02:45,389
And then there's the computer.

49
00:02:46,515 --> 00:02:50,165
Not something that stores data
for seventy-five thousand years.

50
00:02:50,995 --> 00:02:52,765
Something that actually operates.

51
00:02:53,695 --> 00:02:56,335
The processor in your
phone is unimaginably more

52
00:02:56,415 --> 00:02:59,545
powerful than computers
from a few decades ago.

53
00:03:00,213 --> 00:03:03,293
At some point we're asking
engineering to deal with a timescale

54
00:03:03,373 --> 00:03:06,603
for which we have essentially
no practical experience.

55
00:03:08,863 --> 00:03:10,293
And then there's energy.

56
00:03:11,199 --> 00:03:14,039
For decades, radioisotope generators have

57
00:03:14,119 --> 00:03:16,689
been extremely useful
for deep-space missions.

58
00:03:17,503 --> 00:03:20,043
They run on plutonium
two three eight, which

59
00:03:20,123 --> 00:03:23,013
loses half its output
every eighty-eight years.

60
00:03:23,807 --> 00:03:26,987
After a thousand years, a
generator like that is putting

61
00:03:27,067 --> 00:03:29,957
out less than a thousandth
of what it started with.

62
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:34,054
And a thousand years is just
over one percent of the journey.

63
00:03:34,921 --> 00:03:38,691
That's not a small engineering
problem. That's the mission.

64
00:03:39,627 --> 00:03:43,337
You would need a power source that keeps
working for an absurdly long time...

65
00:03:43,747 --> 00:03:48,897
or a spacecraft that can shut itself down
for thousands of years and wake up later.

66
00:03:49,716 --> 00:03:51,596
And now we've turned our spacecraft into

67
00:03:51,676 --> 00:03:53,316
something that looks
less like a satellite...

68
00:03:53,396 --> 00:03:56,926
and more like a
technological time capsule.

69
00:04:00,156 --> 00:04:02,546
Of course, we don't have
to use Voyager's speed.

70
00:04:03,196 --> 00:04:06,486
There are several ways we could
make a much faster spacecraft.

71
00:04:07,507 --> 00:04:11,757
And there is one particularly
crazy idea... laser propulsion.

72
00:04:12,604 --> 00:04:16,304
Instead of carrying the energy on
board, we could put a huge laser array

73
00:04:16,384 --> 00:04:21,394
on Earth and shine an extremely
powerful beam at a tiny reflective sail.

74
00:04:22,172 --> 00:04:23,602
No giant engine.

75
00:04:24,252 --> 00:04:25,682
The laser does the pushing.

76
00:04:26,537 --> 00:04:29,917
Concepts such as Breakthrough
Starshot have proposed accelerating

77
00:04:29,997 --> 00:04:34,627
extremely small spacecraft to twenty
percent of the speed of light.

78
00:04:35,358 --> 00:04:38,648
The laser they have in mind
would draw a hundred gigawatts.

79
00:04:39,618 --> 00:04:44,228
That is nearly three times the entire
electricity demand of the United Kingdom.

80
00:04:44,971 --> 00:04:47,331
At that speed, a probe
could reach the Proxima

81
00:04:47,411 --> 00:04:50,081
Centauri system in roughly twenty years.

82
00:04:50,891 --> 00:04:54,228
Twenty years instead of
seventy-five thousand.

83
00:04:54,677 --> 00:04:57,467
But there's a catch.
Actually... there are several.

84
00:04:59,451 --> 00:05:03,401
Your tiny spacecraft crosses
interstellar space for twenty years.

85
00:05:04,197 --> 00:05:05,397
And then... what?

86
00:05:06,279 --> 00:05:09,469
You might think... "Easy. Just
slow it down when it gets there."

87
00:05:10,173 --> 00:05:12,303
No. That's one of the biggest problems.

88
00:05:13,255 --> 00:05:17,205
Slowing it down means getting rid
of all the energy the laser put in.

89
00:05:18,035 --> 00:05:21,125
And there is no giant laser
waiting for you at Proxima.

90
00:05:21,844 --> 00:05:24,424
So the simplest version
of a Starshot-like mission

91
00:05:24,504 --> 00:05:27,874
is not an expedition
that arrives at Proxima.

92
00:05:28,575 --> 00:05:29,775
It's a flyby.

93
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:32,500
The spacecraft screams
through the system at a

94
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,790
significant fraction of
the speed of light...

95
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:36,720
takes a few pictures...

96
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:39,300
collects whatever data it can...

97
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:42,130
and disappears into interstellar space.

98
00:05:42,974 --> 00:05:46,034
There is no second attempt.
There is no U-turn.

99
00:05:46,114 --> 00:05:47,944
And there is no parking orbit.

100
00:05:50,214 --> 00:05:54,234
And at that speed, space stops
being empty. There is dust.

101
00:05:54,314 --> 00:05:56,584
There are tiny particles.
There is radiation.

102
00:05:57,715 --> 00:06:02,565
At ordinary spacecraft speeds, hitting
a tiny particle may cause some damage.

103
00:06:03,357 --> 00:06:06,407
The biggest specks out there
weigh about a billionth of a gram.

104
00:06:07,577 --> 00:06:09,677
At a fifth of the speed
of light, one of those

105
00:06:09,757 --> 00:06:12,367
arrives with the energy of a rifle bullet.

106
00:06:13,247 --> 00:06:16,737
So it may need shielding.
But shielding has mass.

107
00:06:17,547 --> 00:06:19,567
And mass is exactly what
we don't want, because

108
00:06:19,647 --> 00:06:22,457
every extra gram makes
acceleration harder.

109
00:06:24,637 --> 00:06:29,027
And then there is something that
is easy to overlook. Navigation.

110
00:06:29,919 --> 00:06:32,949
Proxima is moving. So is the Sun.

111
00:06:33,059 --> 00:06:35,289
So is everything else in the galaxy.

112
00:06:36,239 --> 00:06:37,669
But that part is manageable.

113
00:06:38,659 --> 00:06:41,389
Astronomers are very good at
predicting stellar motion.

114
00:06:42,290 --> 00:06:45,630
At twenty percent of the speed
of light, even a tiny error

115
00:06:45,710 --> 00:06:48,620
in the trajectory can become
enormous over four light-years.

116
00:06:49,981 --> 00:06:52,731
And the spacecraft can't
ask Earth for directions.

117
00:06:53,401 --> 00:06:57,051
Because the signal takes more than
four years to cross the distance.

118
00:06:57,929 --> 00:07:00,439
By the time we discover
that something went wrong...

119
00:07:00,849 --> 00:07:03,549
the spacecraft may already
be millions or billions

120
00:07:03,629 --> 00:07:06,599
of kilometres away from
where we wanted it to be.

121
00:07:07,314 --> 00:07:09,164
So it has to navigate itself.

122
00:07:10,074 --> 00:07:13,844
We already use autonomous systems
in spacecraft, but not like this.

123
00:07:14,623 --> 00:07:18,283
It would have to find its own
orientation from the stars, and decide

124
00:07:18,363 --> 00:07:22,273
for itself whether a measurement
is real or a sensor has failed.

125
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:26,590
For a probe travelling to Proxima,
Earth is not mission control.

126
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,430
Earth is a very distant observer.

127
00:07:31,850 --> 00:07:34,520
Let's assume we've somehow
solved all of this.

128
00:07:35,253 --> 00:07:36,743
The spacecraft survives.

129
00:07:37,593 --> 00:07:41,443
It reaches the Proxima Centauri
system. It takes its pictures.

130
00:07:42,478 --> 00:07:44,798
And then it is past the
star, falling away into

131
00:07:44,878 --> 00:07:47,568
the dark with the only
copy of everything it saw.

132
00:07:48,318 --> 00:07:50,448
Now it has to send that home.

133
00:07:51,195 --> 00:07:54,495
A tiny spacecraft has a
tiny transmitter, and it is

134
00:07:54,575 --> 00:07:57,305
trying to reach us across
four light-years of space.

135
00:07:58,203 --> 00:08:00,133
It's like trying to
shout across an ocean...

136
00:08:00,803 --> 00:08:02,273
while both of you are moving...

137
00:08:02,843 --> 00:08:05,233
and you have to hit a target
smaller than a coin...

138
00:08:05,423 --> 00:08:07,073
from four light-years away.

139
00:08:07,937 --> 00:08:11,427
And a complete conversation with
it takes more than eight years.

140
00:08:12,257 --> 00:08:14,007
And then there's the data rate.

141
00:08:14,857 --> 00:08:18,007
Getting a tiny amount of
information home might be possible.

142
00:08:18,905 --> 00:08:22,155
Getting a beautiful high-resolution
movie of Proxima's planets?

143
00:08:23,065 --> 00:08:24,265
That's another matter.

144
00:08:26,865 --> 00:08:29,795
So... can we reach another
star with today's technology?

145
00:08:30,779 --> 00:08:32,389
If by "reach" you mean...

146
00:08:33,139 --> 00:08:35,399
send a piece of hardware
onto a trajectory that

147
00:08:35,479 --> 00:08:38,289
eventually crosses the
Proxima Centauri system?

148
00:08:39,012 --> 00:08:40,462
Then... probably yes.

149
00:08:40,992 --> 00:08:43,482
We already have spacecraft
on their way out.

150
00:08:44,209 --> 00:08:45,839
But if by "reach" you mean...

151
00:08:46,529 --> 00:08:50,799
send a useful scientific spacecraft
to Proxima and have it arrive working?

152
00:08:51,588 --> 00:08:54,078
Then the answer becomes
much less comfortable.

153
00:08:54,969 --> 00:08:58,609
And if you mean... send it there
today, have it work on its own for

154
00:08:58,689 --> 00:09:01,619
tens of thousands of years,
and get the data back?

155
00:09:02,391 --> 00:09:05,561
Then we're no longer talking about
something we know how to build.

156
00:09:08,501 --> 00:09:11,511
And that's what makes interstellar
travel so fascinating.

157
00:09:12,501 --> 00:09:14,651
The hardest part may not be propulsion.

158
00:09:15,481 --> 00:09:17,131
It may not even be the distance.

159
00:09:18,125 --> 00:09:19,325
It may be time.

160
00:09:21,969 --> 00:09:24,659
We know how to build
machines that work for years.

161
00:09:25,209 --> 00:09:27,799
We've built machines
that work for decades.

162
00:09:28,744 --> 00:09:32,134
But an interstellar spacecraft may
need to survive for centuries...

163
00:09:33,084 --> 00:09:37,614
or, with today's relatively slow
propulsion, tens of thousands of years.

164
00:09:38,427 --> 00:09:40,977
That's a completely different
engineering problem.

165
00:09:41,851 --> 00:09:43,671
And perhaps the first truly successful

166
00:09:43,751 --> 00:09:47,261
interstellar spacecraft
won't be the fastest one.

167
00:09:47,921 --> 00:09:50,611
It might be the one designed
around a different idea...

168
00:09:51,281 --> 00:09:52,551
don't make it last forever.

169
00:09:53,487 --> 00:09:56,897
Make it sleep. Let it
travel. Let it wake up.

170
00:09:57,659 --> 00:10:00,669
Check itself. Repair itself. Navigate.

171
00:10:01,704 --> 00:10:03,814
Do the science. And then call home.

172
00:10:06,244 --> 00:10:08,214
So perhaps the question isn't...

173
00:10:08,724 --> 00:10:12,274
"Can we get to Proxima
Centauri?" We probably can.

174
00:10:13,049 --> 00:10:14,479
The real question is...

175
00:10:14,589 --> 00:10:16,989
"Can we build a machine
today that still knows

176
00:10:17,069 --> 00:10:19,299
what it's supposed to
do when it gets there?"

177
00:10:19,981 --> 00:10:23,251
Because reaching another star
isn't just a problem of distance.

178
00:10:24,215 --> 00:10:29,105
It's a problem of speed. Energy.
Navigation. Communication. Reliability.

179
00:10:29,455 --> 00:10:30,655
Autonomy.

180
00:10:30,927 --> 00:10:32,537
And above all... time.

181
00:10:34,946 --> 00:10:37,076
And when you're talking
about another star...

182
00:10:37,666 --> 00:10:40,836
four light-years suddenly doesn't
seem quite so close anymore.
